  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Lecture core · A shallow network needs a nonlinearity",
      "body": "A composition of affine maps is still affine: $W_2(W_1x+b_1)+b_2$ can be collapsed into one affine map. A nonlinear activation between layers prevents that collapse and lets a shallow network form curved or piecewise-linear decision boundaries.",
      "sourceType": "lecture",
      "teaching": {
        "concept": "Without an activation, composing affine layers collapses to one affine map; the activation is what expands the representable function class.",
        "useWhen": "Use it to explain why architecture alone does not create nonlinear modeling power.",
        "examMove": "Multiply two affine maps symbolically, then point to the activation as the step that prevents collapse.",
        "trap": "More linear layers do not replace a nonlinear activation."
      },
      "math": {
        "latex": "h=\\sigma(W_1x+b_1),\\qquad f(x)=W_2h+b_2",
        "explanation": "The activation is the source of extra expressive shape. If $\\sigma$ is the identity everywhere, stacking layers does not create a richer linear decision family.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\sigma",
            "meaning": "nonlinear activation"
          },
          {
            "latex": "h",
            "meaning": "hidden representation"
          },
          {
            "latex": "W_l,b_l",
            "meaning": "layer weights and bias"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Shallow neural-network forward map",
        "purpose": "Use it to show where the nonlinearity enters and why stacked linear maps alone collapse to one linear map.",
        "sourceType": "lecture"
      },
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Gradient descent and non-convexity",
      "body": "Gradient descent updates parameters using the local gradient: $\\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\eta\\nabla L(\\theta_t)$. For a convex objective, every local minimum is global; neural-network objectives are generally non-convex, so backprop computes gradients efficiently but does not promise a global optimum.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\eta\\nabla_\\theta L(\\theta_t)",
        "explanation": "The learning rate controls the step size. A small gradient can mean a stationary point, saturation, or a poorly scaled parameterization; it is not proof that the global best loss has been found.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\theta",
            "meaning": "all trainable parameters"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\eta",
            "meaning": "learning rate"
          },
          {
            "latex": "L",
            "meaning": "training loss"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Gradient-descent parameter update",
        "purpose": "Use it to describe one optimization step and distinguish local update mechanics from global optimality.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      },
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · Backpropagation recurrence",
      "body": "For $z^l=W^lh^{l-1}+b^l$ and $h^l=\\sigma_l(z^l)$, define the error signal $\\delta^l=\\partial L/\\partial z^l$. Starting at the output, apply the chain rule backward; each layer reuses the upstream signal instead of recomputing every path from scratch.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\delta^l=(W^{l+1\\top}\\delta^{l+1})\\odot\\sigma_l'(z^l),\\qquad \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W^l}=\\delta^l h^{l-1\\top}",
        "explanation": "The transpose transports sensitivity from the next layer; the elementwise derivative gates it at the current pre-activation. The outer product gives the parameter gradient for an affine layer.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\delta^l",
            "meaning": "backpropagated pre-activation gradient"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\odot",
            "meaning": "elementwise product"
          },
          {
            "latex": "h^{l-1}",
            "meaning": "input to layer $l$"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Backpropagation error recurrence",
        "purpose": "Use it to propagate local derivatives backward through a computation graph without recomputing every path.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      },
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Assessment habit · Direct differentiation versus backprop",
      "body": "For a tiny scalar network, direct chain-rule expansion is acceptable and often helps verify signs. For a vector network, backprop stores intermediate values and reuses shared subexpressions, reducing repeated work and making the gradient scale with the graph rather than with the number of input-output paths.",
      "sourceType": "ref",
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Textbook depth · L2-regularized neural objective",
      "body": "With $L_{total}=L_{data}+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\sum_l\\lVert W^l\\rVert_F^2$, the data gradient is backpropagated through the network and the regularizer contributes $\\lambda W^l$ directly to each weight gradient. Biases are usually excluded unless the objective says otherwise.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "math": {
        "latex": "\\frac{\\partial L_{total}}{\\partial W^l}=\\frac{\\partial L_{data}}{\\partial W^l}+\\lambda W^l",
        "explanation": "Keep the computational graph for the data term separate from the explicit derivative of the penalty. This is a common place to lose the $\\lambda$ term in an exam derivation.",
        "symbols": [
          {
            "latex": "\\lambda",
            "meaning": "L2 strength"
          },
          {
            "latex": "\\lVert W^l\\rVert_F",
            "meaning": "Frobenius norm of layer weights"
          }
        ],
        "name": "Regularized neural objective",
        "purpose": "Use it to balance data fit against parameter magnitude when training a flexible network.",
        "sourceType": "textbook"
      },
      "sourceRefs": []
    }
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "title": "Scalar chain rule",
      "steps": [
        "Let $u=wx$, $h=\\operatorname{ReLU}(u)$, $p=\\sigma(h)$, and $L=\\frac12(p-t)^2$. For $x=1,t=0,w=2$, $u=h=2$ and $p\\approx0.881$.",
        "The local derivatives are $\\partial L/\\partial p=p-t\\approx0.881$, $\\sigma'(h)=p(1-p)\\approx0.105$, and $\\operatorname{ReLU}'(u)=1$.",
        "Therefore $\\partial L/\\partial w\\approx0.881\\times0.105\\times1\\times x\\approx0.0925$."
      ],
      "answer": "A scalar graph is a good sign-checking exercise; the same local factors are reused in vector backprop.",
      "sourceType": "textbook",
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Linear activations collapse",
      "steps": [
        "Compose two layers with identity activations: $h=W_1x+b_1$ and $f=W_2h+b_2$.",
        "Expand: $f=(W_2W_1)x+(W_2b_1+b_2)$, another affine function.",
        "Therefore an exam statement that a deep stack of purely linear layers creates a nonlinear boundary is false."
      ],
      "answer": "Nonlinear activations are structural, not an optional decoration.",
      "sourceType": "lecture",
      "sourceRefs": []
    }
  ],
  "criticalQuestions": [
    {
      "prompt": "What does backprop guarantee?",
      "focus": "optimization versus differentiation",
      "angle": "separate gradient computation from search",
      "modelAnswer": "Backprop computes the gradient of the chosen loss efficiently. It does not guarantee that gradient descent reaches a global optimum for a non-convex neural-network objective.",
      "sourceRefs": []
    },
    {
      "prompt": "Why is the forward pass needed before the backward pass?",
      "focus": "computational graph",
      "angle": "reuse activation values",
      "modelAnswer": "Local derivatives depend on intermediate pre-activations and activations. The forward pass records these values so the backward pass can multiply local Jacobian factors in reverse order.",
      "sourceRefs": []
    }
  ],
